          "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"UNPERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"The entry strongly captures the ethical use of righteousness in Deuteronomy 6:25, Psalm 119:172, and 1 John 3:7, but it presents “right-living” and commandments enacted as the term’s defining sense without qualification. That does not adequately explain Romans 4:4–8, where righteousness is counted to an ungodly person “without works” and is explicated through forgiveness and non-imputation of sin, or Philippians 3:9, where Paul contrasts his law-derived righteousness with righteousness received from God through faith. The chapter’s distinction between wage-earning works and obedient works is useful, but the immediate mechanics of Romans 4 remain juridical and imputational: God changes the person’s standing rather than calling performed commandments righteousness. A context-sensitive account—rightness according to God’s standard, expressed either as right conduct or as a favorable standing granted by God—explains both groups of texts with less strain.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Righteousness is enacted right conduct—specifically, for persons under God’s authority, His commandments lived out—rather than primarily a conferred or credited standing.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"God is Right-Living by definition.\",\"The opposite of righteousness is sin.\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[\"The unqualified identification of righteousness with “Right-living.”\",\"The claim that, for those under God’s authority, righteousness simply is God’s commandments lived out.\"],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"The chapter’s claim that Romans 4 excludes only deeds presented as wage claims does not fully account for Paul’s broader language of righteousness imputed “without works,” justification of the ungodly, forgiveness, and non-imputation of sin.\",\"The chapter’s further identification of the wedding garment with commandment-keeping in every relevant respect exceeds what is asserted in the glossary entry and would require separate evaluation.\",\"The broader claims that Christ as the law’s telos establishes continuing commandment observance, and that Pauline law texts uniformly preserve the book’s conception of right-living, are chapter-level arguments rather than glossary assertions.\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"Righteousness denotes being right according to God’s standard. Depending on context, it can describe righteous conduct and covenant obedience, God’s own just action or character, or a favorable standing that God grants and credits through faith apart from works. Commandment-keeping is therefore a genuine manifestation of righteousness, but not its exhaustive definition.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"In Romans 4 and Philippians 3, righteousness is a God-granted, credited right standing associated with forgiveness and faith, not the recipient’s commandments already lived out.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The same righteousness cannot, in Romans 4’s immediate argument, be both commandments performed by the recipient and righteousness credited to the ungodly without works. The book may argue that granted righteousness later produces obedience, but that does not establish that the righteousness being imputed is itself those enacted commandments.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Deuteronomy 6:25 directly supports an ethical-covenantal use: observing the commandments would be Israel’s righteousness.\",\"Psalm 119:172 likewise calls God’s commandments righteousness, supporting their status as the standard of right conduct.\",\"First John 3:7 links being righteous with doing righteousness, confirming that conduct genuinely manifests righteousness.\",\"Romans 4:5 identifies the recipient as ungodly and says faith is counted for righteousness; verses 6–8 explain the blessing as righteousness imputed without works, sins forgiven, and sin not imputed.\",\"Philippians 3:9 contrasts Paul’s own law-derived righteousness with righteousness from God through faith, which is more naturally a received standing than his commandments lived out.\",\"The counter-reading preserves the direct ethical texts while also allowing the forensic mechanics explicitly present in the Pauline texts; the glossary’s uniform equation does not.\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It closely follows the explicit wording of Deuteronomy 6:25 and Psalm 119:172.\",\"It explains the active relationship between doing righteousness and being righteous in 1 John 3:7.\",\"It gives a coherent account of divine righteousness as visible in God’s right ways and works in Deuteronomy 32:4 and Psalm 145:17.\",\"It rightly resists treating righteousness as a status wholly disconnected from transformed conduct.\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It accounts for both ethical and forensic uses rather than forcing every occurrence into conduct.\",\"It explains why Romans 4 emphasizes counting, imputing, forgiveness, non-imputation, the ungodly, and absence of works.\",\"It explains Paul’s contrast between his own law-derived righteousness and righteousness received from God in Philippians 3:9.\",\"It can affirm every clear conduct text cited by the entry without equating those contextual uses with the term’s exhaustive meaning.\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[\"Romans 4:5 calls the person whom God justifies “ungodly” while saying his faith is counted for righteousness.\",\"Romans 4:6 expressly describes God as imputing righteousness “without works.”\",\"Romans 4:7–8 interprets the blessed standing through forgiveness and non-imputation of sin, which are judicial acts rather than commandments performed.\",\"Philippians 3:9 locates the relevant righteousness outside Paul’s own law-derived righteousness and says it is from God through faith.\"],\"comparative_winner\":\"COUNTER\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"Romans 4:4–8\",\"Philippians 3:9\",\"Deuteronomy 6:25\",\"Psalm 119:172\",\"1 John 3:7\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"The precise mechanism of credited righteousness—imputation of Christ’s righteousness, covenant status, participatory union, or another account—is not settled by the supplied excerpts.\",\"The ethical and forensic senses may be theologically integrated, but the evidence does not establish the glossary’s reduction of both to commandments lived out.\",\"The entry’s “Opposite: Sin” is broadly defensible in the moral register, although it is less exact for every divine, saving, or forensic use of righteousness.\"],\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
